# config/subsys/Kconfig.drivers — Hardware driver enables and per-driver placement
#
# Each option maps to the corresponding driver being
# compiled in.  Board dependencies are encoded via `depends on BOARD_*`.

config E1000E_EXT_DESC
    bool "e1000e extended descriptors (RFCTL.EXTEN receive, DEXT transmit)"
    default y
    help
      crates/drivers/net/src/e1000e.rs. y: the receive ring uses the
      extended write-back format (status and error word, 16-bit length and
      the RSS/checksum words the controller fills) and the transmit ring
      uses extended data descriptors (20-bit length). n: the legacy 82543
      formats on both. The frames on the wire are the same; this picks which
      of the two layouts the controller is told to speak.

config E1000E_LINK_WAIT_MS
    int "e1000e: milliseconds to wait for link at bring-up (0 = do not wait)"
    range 0 10000
    default 2000
    help
      crates/drivers/net/src/e1000e.rs: after the controller is reset and
      the link is set up, bring-up waits this long for STATUS.LU. A cable
      that is not plugged in costs the whole wait once at boot; the NIC is
      then up with its link down and frames are refused until the link
      comes (a link-status interrupt, or the next poll).

config IGC_LINK_WAIT_MS
    int "igc: milliseconds to wait for link at bring-up (0 = do not wait)"
    range 0 10000
    default 4000
    help
      crates/drivers/net/src/igc.rs: after the controller is reset and the
      link is set up, bring-up waits this long for STATUS.LU. The I225
      negotiates 2.5 Gb/s in two to four seconds; a cable that is not
      plugged in costs the whole wait once at boot, and the NIC is then up
      with its link down: frames are refused until the link comes (a
      link-status interrupt, or the next poll).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Ring-3 driver proxy
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config PROXY_SPIN_US
    int "Ring-3 driver proxy: spin before blocking on a reply (us)"
    range 0 1000
    default 20
    help
      How long an in-kernel client of a ring-3 driver polls for the reply
      before it blocks, and only while the driver is running on another
      hart (crates/drivers/sys/src/user_driver_proxy.rs, PROXY_SPIN_US;
      crates/drivers/driver_server/src/reply_wait.rs, wait_for_reply). 0 disables
      the spin: every wait blocks at once.

      The right value is board-specific: it should sit just above the time
      a running driver takes to answer, and below what a block + cross-hart
      wake + switch back costs on that board. Measure it on the board, not
      under QEMU (-icount serialises the vCPUs, so the spin cannot help there
      at all):
        1. Boot the qemu-style image whose autorun is gpio_drv (the image the
           "userspace: ring-3 GPIO driver" gate row builds) with -smp >= 2
           and read the kernel line
             [AQ3] proxy round trip over 32 pings: min=.. median=..us max=.. failed=0 spin=N
           `spin=N` counts the replies the spin found before any block.
        2. Rebuild with PROXY_SPIN_US=0 and repeat: that median is the cost
           of the block path on this board.
        3. Raise the value until `spin=` stops growing; keep the smallest
           value that finds most replies while its median stays below the
           step-2 median. Several boots per value: the numbers are wall
           clock.
      20 us is the value measured on QEMU TCG (MTTCG, 4 harts): round trip
      medians 11-12 us with the spin vs 47-69 us without, 24-31 of 32
      replies found by the spin (wave 9).

config PROXY_REPLY_TIMEOUT_MS
    int "Ring-3 driver proxy: reply timeout (ms)"
    range 1 10000
    default 100
    help
      How long an in-kernel client of a ring-3 driver waits, blocked, for
      the reply before the request fails with DriverError::Busy
      (crates/drivers/sys/src/user_driver_proxy.rs,
      PROXY_REPLY_TIMEOUT_MS).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Hardware bus
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

choice HWBUS_AUTOBIND
    prompt "Hardware bus: which detected devices get a driver (HWBUS_AUTOBIND)"
    default HWBUS_AUTOBIND_TOPOLOGY if PRESET_ROBOT || PRESET_INDUSTRIAL || PRESET_AUTOMOTIVE
    default HWBUS_AUTOBIND_TOPOLOGY if PRESET_FIRMWARE || PRESET_WEARABLE
    default HWBUS_AUTOBIND_AUTO
    help
      The hardware bus (crates/drivers/hwbus, azos_drv_bus::hwbus)
      enumerates PCI/PCIe functions, device-tree nodes that carry a
      `compatible` and populated virtio-mmio windows into device objects,
      each named by a `Device` capability. This says which of them get a
      driver. Either way every device is detected and listed in the
      introspection tree's `devices/`; the choice is only about binding.

      Presets: desktop, server, IoT/HMI, gateway and generic bind
      automatically; robot, industrial, automotive, firmware and wearable
      bind only what the signed topology declares.

    config HWBUS_AUTOBIND_AUTO
        bool "Auto: bind every device a driver table matches"
        help
          The native table is consulted first, then the Linux table (large
          boards only: HWBUS binds a Linux class only when the image has
          LINUX_DRIVERS); the first match is bound. A `[device.*]` row of
          the topology may still name another native driver or refuse a
          device.

    config HWBUS_AUTOBIND_TOPOLOGY
        bool "Topology only: bind what the signed topology admits"
        help
          Everything is detected and nothing is bound unless a
          `[device.<name>]` row of the topology admits it (`match =
          "<selector>"`). A device a driver table matches but no row
          admits is not touched; the bus records the decision "detected,
          not admitted" (`why list`). The hardware an image needs to
          boot (the console, the interrupt controller, the timer) is
          brought up before the bus and is not subject to this; the
          built-in topology of each image admits the devices that image's
          own drivers need (its disk and network interface).

endchoice

config UART_16550_TX_FIFO_DEPTH
    int "Console: 16550 transmit FIFO depth (bytes per THR-empty)"
    range 1 256
    default 16
    help
      crates/drivers/sys/src/uart.rs, TX_FIFO_DEPTH of the MMIO 16550
      (riscv64 QEMU virt, VF2/K1) and of the x86_64 port-I/O COM1. With
      the FIFOs on, THR-empty means the whole FIFO is free, so this many
      bytes go out per line-status read on the synchronous path and per
      TX interrupt on the ring path. 16 is the 16550A (and QEMU's model);
      a 16750 or a DesignWare APB UART built with a deeper FIFO can take
      64 or more. Too large overruns the FIFO and drops console bytes; too
      small costs one more status read (an MMIO access or, on x86_64, a
      VM exit under a hypervisor) and one more interrupt per that many
      bytes. Costs no RAM. Unused by the PL011 (aarch64).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional drivers and buses (each maps to a kernel cargo feature)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config DRV_PCI
    # cargo-feature: pci
    bool "PCI bus: ECAM enumeration, MSI-X, virtio-pci transport"
    default n
    depends on BOARD_QEMU || BOARD_VF2 || BOARD_RPI5
    help
      Kernel feature `pci` (crates/drivers/pci; virtio-pci modern transport
      for virtio-net). QEMU, and the boards whose PCIe controller PCI_SOC_HOST
      brings up. riscv64 and aarch64 `virt`: ECAM from the DTB. x86_64: ECAM from the ACPI MCFG (q35), else
      X86_PCI_ECAM_BASE (QEMU microvm `pcie=on`: no MCFG and no ports),
      else the legacy 0xCF8/0xCFC port pair; MSI
      and MSI-X go straight to the local APIC on a vector from the
      MSI block above the IOAPIC lines (X86_MSI_VECTORS). Built and
      booted by the gate's PCI rows. n: the PCI code is not compiled.

config PCI_MAX_FUNCTIONS
    int "Most PCI functions the boot enumeration keeps"
    range 8 256
    default 64 if ARCH_X86_64
    default 32
    help
      The capacity of the table the boot enumeration fills
      (azos_pci::enumerate_tree, kernel/src/main.rs): every function on
      bus 0 and behind the bridges, in bus order. A function past it is
      counted and said ("[PCI] dropped N function(s)") and no driver sees
      it. QEMU's machines need single digits; a PC is denser (a desktop
      chipset's root ports, bridges and multi-function devices reach
      40-60), hence x86_64's 64.

      Cost: about 250 bytes of the boot stack per function while the boot
      enumerates (16 KiB at 64; the boot stack is 256 KiB); nothing after.
      Not under DRV_PCI: `make qemu-pci` and the gate's ad-hoc PCI kernels
      build the cargo feature `pci` on a config without it, and the
      enumeration reads this constant.

config PCI_SOC_HOST
    # cargo-feature: pci-soc-host
    bool "SoC PCIe host controllers: JH7110 (PLDA) and BCM2712 (brcmstb)"
    depends on DRV_PCI && SOC_FRAMEWORKS && (BOARD_VF2 || BOARD_RPI5)
    default y
    help
      Emits the kernel cargo feature `pci-soc-host`. The PCIe controllers of
      the VisionFive 2 and the Raspberry Pi 5 are not brought up by their
      firmware: before the enumeration the kernel finds the bridge node by its
      `compatible` (`starfive,jh7110-pcie`, `brcm,bcm2712-pcie`), takes the
      clocks, resets and pin state it names from the SoC frameworks
      (SOC_FRAMEWORKS), holds the endpoint in reset, programs the windows from
      the node's `ranges` and `dma-ranges`, releases PERST# and waits for the
      link (crates/drivers/pci/src/host, kernel/src/boot/pci.rs). The VF2's
      PERST# is a GPIO of the SYS pad controller (`perst-gpios`). A link that
      stays down is a boot line and no enumeration. The JH7110's PCIe clocks
      and PHY are not modelled by the frameworks and are left as the
      firmware set them. n: the bridge is not brought up and enumeration
      finds nothing.

config DRV_NVME
    # cargo-feature: nvme
    bool "NVMe SSD over PCIe (native driver, admin + an I/O queue pair per CPU)"
    default n
    depends on DRV_PCI
    help
      Kernel feature `nvme` (crates/drivers/block/src/nvme.rs over the
      wire-protocol crate crates/drivers/nvme). Finds an NVMe controller
      (PCI class 01:08:02) on ECAM bus 0 before the block device is
      brought up, resets and enables it, identifies the controller and
      namespace 1, creates the I/O submission/completion queue pairs
      (NVME_QUEUES, one per CPU up to that) and becomes the boot block
      device in place of virtio-blk, so the FAT32 volume mounts from it.
      Reads and writes carry PRP lists (a transfer of up to NVME_SLOT_KB
      per command), Flush is sent where the controller reports a volatile
      write cache. Completions come by MSI-X where the machine routes it
      (riscv64 AIA IMSIC, aarch64 GICv3 ITS, x86_64 local APIC) and are
      polled otherwise. Each queue pair has its
      own lock, held only for bookkeeping, never across the device wait, and
      its own MSI-X vector; NVME_INFLIGHT commands are in the device at
      once, spread over the queues. 512-byte-block namespaces only. QEMU:
      `-drive file=disk.img,if=none,id=nv -device nvme,serial=x,drive=nv`.
      n: the driver is not compiled and virtio-blk is the block device.

config DRV_AHCI
    # cargo-feature: ahci
    bool "SATA disk over PCIe (native AHCI 1.3 host)"
    default n
    depends on DRV_PCI
    help
      Kernel feature `ahci` (crates/drivers/block/src/ahci.rs). Binds by PCI
      class (01:06:01) through the hardware bus
      (crates/drivers/block/hwbus.ids) before the block device is brought
      up: takes the controller from the firmware, resets it, finds the first
      port with an ATA disk behind it (spin-up and link wait bounded),
      identifies the disk and becomes the boot block device in place of
      virtio-blk, so the FAT32 volume mounts from it. Where the HBA and the
      disk queue (CAP.SNCQ, IDENTIFY word 76), up to AHCI_NCQ_DEPTH reads and
      writes are in the disk at once as READ/WRITE FPDMA QUEUED, and an NCQ
      error is recovered through the NCQ error log (READ LOG EXT 10h): the
      failed command fails, the others are sent again; otherwise one command
      is in the disk at a time as READ/WRITE DMA EXT. Each command slot has
      a command table whose PRDT names its staging pages (a transfer of up
      to AHCI_XFER_KB per command). FLUSH CACHE EXT where the disk has it,
      after the queue drained. A write the block layer asks to force to the
      medium (BLK_FUA's fsync commit) is WRITE FPDMA QUEUED with its FUA bit,
      or WRITE DMA FUA EXT without NCQ, where IDENTIFY word 84 says the disk
      has FUA; otherwise the layer orders with a flush. A discard (BLK_DISCARD:
      FAT32's freed chains, azvol's freed blocks, the swap area) is DATA SET
      MANAGEMENT with the TRIM bit where IDENTIFY word 169 says the disk has
      it, after the queue drained; otherwise the layer refuses it. Completions come by MSI where the machine routes it
      (riscv64 AIA IMSIC, aarch64 GICv3 ITS, x86_64 local APIC) and are
      polled otherwise; legacy INTx is not routed. A command that misses its
      budget resets the controller DEVICE_TIMEOUT_RESETS times per boot (the
      late command fails, the others it held end as reset), then latches
      the driver dead until reboot; a port error restarts the port and fails
      the command. 512-byte logical
      sectors and LBA48 disks only. QEMU (q35): `-device ich9-ahci,id=ahci
      -device ide-hd,drive=d,bus=ahci.0`.
      n: the driver is not compiled and virtio-blk is the block device.

config DRV_E1000E
    # cargo-feature: e1000e
    bool "Intel e1000e Ethernet (82574L, I217/I218/I219) over PCIe"
    default n
    depends on DRV_PCI
    help
      Kernel feature `e1000e` (crates/drivers/net/src/e1000e.rs). Binds by
      PCI id through the hardware bus (crates/drivers/net/hwbus.ids) and
      serves the NIC table of net_device next to board Ethernet and
      virtio-net. One RX and one TX ring (E1000E_RX_DESCS, E1000E_TX_DESCS)
      in the kernel's own memory, 2 KiB buffers, the MAC read from RAL0/RAH0
      as the controller's NVM load left it, link read from STATUS. Receive
      interrupts come as one MSI where the machine routes it (riscv64 AIA
      IMSIC, aarch64 GICv3 ITS, x86_64 local APIC) and are polled
      otherwise. No offloads, no jumbo frames, one queue for every CPU.
      Validated on the QEMU model only (`-device e1000e,netdev=...`, an
      82574L); the I219 needs the PHY/ME bring-up of a real board, which is
      not done. n: the driver is not compiled and the NIC table holds board
      Ethernet and virtio-net only.

config WDT_SBSA
    # cargo-feature: wdt-sbsa
    bool "Arm SBSA Generic Watchdog"
    default y if ARCH_AARCH64
    default n
    depends on ARCH_AARCH64
    help
      Kernel feature `wdt-sbsa` (crates/drivers/sys/src/wdt_sbsa.rs,
      crates/drivers/sys/src/wdt.rs). The watchdog of Arm server-class and
      BSA machines, found by the hardware bus as a device-tree node
      `arm,sbsa-gwdt` (two `reg` frames: control, then refresh): it becomes
      the hardware watchdog sys-wdt arms and the RT tick kicks. The counter
      runs at the live CNTFRQ; the reset comes at the second expiry (WS1),
      so the offset register holds half the timeout. The ACPI description
      (GTDT) is not read: the aarch64 boot has no ACPI table walk. QEMU
      `virt` has no such watchdog (only `sbsa-ref`, through firmware ACPI).
      n: such a machine has the software watchdog only.

config WDT_SP5100_TCO
    # cargo-feature: wdt-sp5100
    bool "AMD FCH watchdog (SP5100 TCO) on Zen chipsets"
    default y if ARCH_X86_64
    default n
    depends on ARCH_X86_64 && DRV_PCI
    help
      Kernel feature `wdt-sp5100` (crates/drivers/sys/src/wdt_sp5100.rs,
      crates/drivers/sys/src/wdt.rs). The watchdog in the Fusion Controller
      Hub of AMD Zen machines (the Ryzen boards), found by the hardware bus
      through the FCH's SMBus function (PCI 1022:790b, revision 0x51 or
      later; Hygon 1d94:790b): the PM registers turn on the decode of its
      window and select the 1 s resolution, its action is set to reset,
      and it becomes the hardware watchdog sys-wdt arms and the RT tick
      kicks (65535 s at most, whole seconds). Older SB800/SP5100 layouts
      (index/data ports) are not driven. QEMU models no FCH. n: such a
      machine has the software watchdog only.

config WDT_BCM2835_PM
    # cargo-feature: wdt-bcm2835-pm
    bool "Raspberry Pi PM block: hardware watchdog, reboot and power-off (BCM2835/6/7)"
    default y if ARCH_AARCH64 && !HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default n
    depends on ARCH_AARCH64
    help
      Kernel feature `wdt-bcm2835-pm` (crates/drivers/power/src/bcm2835_pm.rs,
      crates/drivers/sys/src/wdt.rs). The power-management block of the
      32-bit-bus Raspberry Pis (the Pi 3 B's BCM2837 among them), found by
      the hardware bus as a device-tree node `brcm,bcm2835-pm` or
      `brcm,bcm2835-pm-wdt` (its first `reg`), or the Pi 5's
      `brcm,bcm2712-pm` (the same block, which Linux drives with the same
      bcm2835_wdt driver; the Pi 5's firmware has PSCI, so it keeps PSCI for
      reboot and power-off): its watchdog becomes the
      hardware watchdog sys-wdt arms and the RT tick kicks (16 s at most;
      a longer timeout is armed as 16 s), and it is how such a Pi reboots
      and powers off. Their firmware has no PSCI, so with the node bound and
      no `/psci` node in the tree, a reboot (the power service, the shell,
      a panic, OTA) resets the SoC through the watchdog, and a power-off
      resets it into boot partition 63, where the firmware halts (the board
      stays powered with everything stopped, as on Linux). A tree that names
      a PSCI conduit keeps PSCI. QEMU `-M raspi3b` models the block (a
      reboot resets the machine; its power-off resets it too, QEMU has no
      halt). n: a Pi has no hardware watchdog and its reboot and power-off
      call a PSCI that is not there.

config RPI_FIRMWARE
    # cargo-feature: rpi-firmware
    bool "Raspberry Pi VideoCore firmware mailbox (power domains, and thermal)"
    default y if ARCH_AARCH64 && !HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    depends on ARCH_AARCH64
    help
      Kernel feature `rpi-firmware` (crates/drivers/firmware,
      kernel/src/entry/aarch64/ext/rpi_mbox.rs). The hardware bus binds the
      device tree's `brcm,bcm2835-mbox` node, the property channel of the
      VideoCore firmware, which on a Raspberry Pi owns what the ARM cores do
      not: the power of the SoC's blocks (a `power-domains` entry of the
      `raspberrypi,bcm2835-power` provider is switched on through it before
      its device's probe: the USB host, which is off until then) and, with
      THERMAL, the SoC temperature and the ARM clock. The firmware's DMA uses
      the bus window of the SoC the tree's root names (azos_firmware
      BusWindow); a SoC whose map is not known never gets it. QEMU `-M
      raspi3b` models the property channel. n: a Pi's USB host stays off.

config WDT_BCM2835_PM_ARM
    # cargo-feature: wdt-bcm2835-pm-arm
    bool "Raspberry Pi PM block: arm it as the hardware watchdog"
    default y
    depends on WDT_BCM2835_PM
    help
      Kernel feature `wdt-bcm2835-pm-arm` (crates/drivers/sys/src/wdt.rs).
      y: the PM block bound by WDT_BCM2835_PM is the hardware watchdog
      sys-wdt arms and the RT tick kicks. n: it is only the reboot and
      power-off of a Pi without PSCI, and no hardware watchdog is armed (the
      software watchdog still runs). QEMU `-M raspi3b` needs n: its PM model
      resets the machine on the arming write itself (it has no countdown),
      so an armed watchdog reboots it at the scheduler's start, as the
      gate's raspi3b rows build (tools/ci_check.sh); a real Pi keeps y.

config DRV_IGC
    # cargo-feature: igc
    bool "Intel I225/I226 2.5 GbE Ethernet (igc) over PCIe"
    default n
    depends on DRV_PCI
    help
      Kernel feature `igc` (crates/drivers/net/src/igc.rs). The 2.5 GbE
      controllers of current Intel and Ryzen desktop and mini-PC boards
      (I225-LM/V/IT/K, I226-LM/V/IT/K, I220-V, I221-V), and QEMU's `-device
      igb` (an 82576, same descriptor format; its interrupt registers sit
      at 0xc0, not 0x1500, and are picked by device id) for the gate. QEMU
      has no I225 model. Binds by PCI id
      through the hardware bus (crates/drivers/net/hwbus.ids) and serves the
      NIC table of net_device next to board Ethernet, e1000e and
      virtio-net. One RX and one TX ring (IGC_RX_DESCS, IGC_TX_DESCS) of
      advanced descriptors in the kernel's own memory, 2 KiB buffers, the
      MAC read from RAL0/RAH0 as the controller's NVM load left it, link
      (10/100/1000/2500) read from STATUS. Receive interrupts come as one
      MSI where the machine routes it (riscv64 AIA IMSIC, aarch64 GICv3 ITS,
      x86_64 local APIC) and are polled otherwise. No offloads, no jumbo
      frames, no RSS, one queue for every CPU. The register offsets and bit
      fields are checked against the Linux igc and igb headers; booted only
      on QEMU's igb model so far. The I225 needs the PHY power-up and
      packet-buffer setup of a real board, which is not done. n: the driver
      is not compiled and the NIC table holds board Ethernet, e1000e and
      virtio-net only.

config DRV_VIRTIO_INPUT
    # cargo-feature: virtio-input base
    bool "virtio-input keyboards and mice (QEMU)"
    default n if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default y
    help
      Kernel feature `virtio-input` (crates/drivers/virtio/src/virtio/
      input.rs, wired by kernel/src/boot/input_irq.rs): finds virtio-input
      devices in the virtio-mmio window (QEMU `-device virtio-keyboard-device`
      / `virtio-mouse-device` / `virtio-tablet-device`, `-device
      virtio-keyboard-pci` is not driven) and takes their events by
      interrupt on riscv64, aarch64 and x86_64 (microvm). A keyboard's key
      presses become bytes in the console's RX ring (US keymap, shift, ctrl,
      caps lock, Enter, Backspace), read by the same single owner of console
      input as the UART: the shell works with no UART input. Mouse and
      tablet events, the wheel, the modifier keys and keys with no byte
      (arrows, F-keys) are published into the input event ring that the
      ring-3 terminal server drains (arrows become escape sequences; the
      mouse selects, pastes and scrolls the framebuffer console as gpm
      does). With no such device on
      the machine nothing prints and nothing changes; a board with no
      virtio-mmio window (VF2, K1) compiles the driver as a stub that
      touches no address. n: the boot wiring and the interrupt arms are not
      compiled. Off by default on a small board (HWCLASS_EMBEDDED), which
      starts only the drivers its topology names; its defconfig sets it
      to ask for one.

config DRV_VIRTIO_SND
    # cargo-feature: virtio-snd base
    bool "virtio-sound audio device (QEMU)"
    default n if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default y
    help
      Kernel feature `virtio-snd` (crates/drivers/virtio/src/virtio/snd.rs,
      virtio spec 1.2 section 5.14, wired by kernel/src/boot/snd_irq.rs and
      served by crates/core/syscall/src/sound_host.rs): finds the virtio
      sound device in the virtio-mmio window (QEMU `-device
      virtio-sound-device`) or on the PCI bus (`-device virtio-sound-pci`,
      with DRV_PCI; one MSI-X vector for the control, transmit and receive
      queues), reads its PCM streams, jacks and channel maps, and serves the
      kernel's `Sound` interface (the endpoint `sound.0`, azos_devif::sound)
      over its one playback stream and one capture stream: a client opens a
      stream on an arena and two queues of period slots, posts periods and
      drains. The device DMAs from or into the client's arena itself (a
      Contig memory object the kernel pins for the stream's life), the
      completions come by interrupt, and the interrupt handler only wakes
      the device pump (`dev-pump`). A capture stream also needs AUDIO_CAPTURE
      and the `mic` effect of the caller's class (config/subsys/Kconfig.audio). With
      no such device nothing prints and nothing changes. n: the driver's
      boot wiring, the interrupt arms and the `Sound` endpoint are not
      compiled. Off by default on a small board (HWCLASS_EMBEDDED), which
      has no virtual hardware: native drivers only.

      Cost: the driver's static state, one control queue and two stream
      queues of 16 entries plus a few request lines, in RAM (the
      `Device` singleton); the host's two stream records. Not measured yet.

config DRV_PS2
    # cargo-feature: ps2 base
    bool "PS/2 keyboard and mouse (i8042 controller, PC)"
    depends on ARCH_X86_64
    default n if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default n if USE_ROBOT || USE_INDUSTRIAL
    default y
    help
      Kernel feature `ps2` (crates/drivers/input/src/ps2.rs over the pure
      controller, scancode and packet code beside it, wired by
      kernel/src/boot/ps2_irq.rs): a PC with no virtio keyboard gets
      console input. The boot looks for the controller only where the ACPI
      FADT says there is one (IAPC_BOOT_ARCH bit 1, the 8042 flag; an ACPI
      1.0 table has no such flag and is taken as a PC AT) and then runs
      the controller's own self-test; a machine with neither (QEMU
      microvm, a hardware-reduced platform) skips it with one boot line
      and nothing else changes. The keyboard is put on scancode set 2 and
      decoded (US keymap, shift, ctrl, caps lock, Enter, Backspace) into
      bytes in the console's RX ring, the same ring as the UART and
      virtio-input; the mouse's three-byte packets (four with a wheel: the
      bring-up asks the mouse for it) become relative-axis, wheel and button
      events. Keys with no byte, the modifier keys and every mouse event are
      published into the ring shared with virtio-input, which the ring-3
      terminal server drains. IRQ1 and IRQ12 arrive through
      the IOAPIC (the MADT overrides honoured) on the boot CPU. Default y
      except for the Robot and Industrial domains, which are not machines
      with a keyboard, and for a small board (HWCLASS_EMBEDDED). A USB keyboard reaches this driver only through
      the firmware's legacy PS/2 emulation, which the same FADT flag
      announces; the native USB keyboard is the USB_HID driver, which ends
      in the same two rings. n: the boot wiring and the interrupt arm are
      not compiled.

config PS2_WAIT_SPINS
    int "PS/2: status reads a controller wait gives up after"
    range 1000 10000000
    default 100000
    help
      crates/drivers/input/src/i8042.rs. Every step of the controller
      bring-up (it takes a command, answers a self-test, a device
      acknowledges) polls the status port at most this many times; the
      result of a device reset (its self-test runs first) waits 8 times as
      many. A status read is one port I/O
      (about a microsecond on a real ISA-compatible bus, a VM exit under a
      hypervisor), so the default is 100 ms per step on hardware. A machine
      with no controller answers 0xFF and fails at once without waiting.
      Too small fails a slow controller or keyboard and leaves the PC with
      no keyboard; too large only lengthens a boot that had no controller
      to find. Costs no RAM. The wait is bounded in reads, not in time, so
      it moves with the bus.

config DRV_RTC
    # cargo-feature: rtc base
    bool "Real-time clock (seeds the wall clock at boot, written back by a reference)"
    default n if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default y
    help
      Kernel feature `rtc` (crates/drivers/rtc, wired by kernel/src/boot/
      rtc.rs): the board's battery-backed clock. At boot, before the
      network, the kernel reads it once and sets the wall clock (REAL, and
      TAI with it) from it: a recorded step (`Rule::ClockStep`), so the
      records of the boot carry a date. The clock discipline is not told:
      the first NTP or PTP sample still steps without confirming (an RTC is
      minutes, or years, off) and `Clock::status` says STATE_SEEDED until
      then. A time the chip itself calls lost (a dead battery, a stopped
      oscillator) or that is before RTC_FLOOR_YEAR is not used. When a
      reference steps the wall clock the kernel writes the chip back, so
      the next boot starts from a time a reference gave. The `Clock`
      interface's `rtc_read` and `rtc_set` are the chip itself for a task;
      a set is an effect of kind KIND_CLOCK (recorded before the chip is
      written when EFFECT_WRITE_AHEAD is on).

      Chips: the QEMU goldfish RTC (riscv64) and the PL031 (aarch64
      `virt`), found on the hardware bus by their compatible string and
      left unbound, so a task holding the device capability can still map
      the window; the MC146818 CMOS clock (x86_64, ports 0x70/0x71; a machine
      without one leaves them floating and the driver says so); the PCF8563 with
      DRV_RTC_PCF8563. A machine without one starts with the wall clock
      unset, as before. A small board (HWCLASS_EMBEDDED) starts only the
      drivers its topology names: its defconfig names DRV_RTC (and the chip)
      when the board has one. Costs about 2 KiB of .text and no RAM.

config RTC_FLOOR_YEAR
    int "RTC: earliest year the kernel believes"
    depends on DRV_RTC
    range 1971 2200
    default 2026
    help
      A time read from the RTC before 1 January of this year is not used:
      a battery that died resets most chips to 2000 or 1970, and a clock
      nobody ever set says the same; a wall clock seeded with it would be
      years wrong where "unset" is honest. The wall clock then stays unset
      until a reference sets it, and the reference's step writes the chip
      back. Raise it with the release; it costs nothing.

config RTC_UIP_POLLS
    int "RTC: status reads a CMOS update wait gives up after"
    depends on DRV_RTC && ARCH_X86_64
    range 1000 10000000
    default 20000
    help
      crates/drivers/rtc/src/mc146818.rs. The MC146818 raises its
      update-in-progress bit 244 us before it rewrites the time, and a read
      waits for the bit to clear. A status read is one index write and one
      data read (about two microseconds on a real bus, a VM exit each under
      a hypervisor), so the default is tens of milliseconds, far over the
      244 us. Too small fails a slow chip and the clock starts unset; too
      large only lengthens a boot whose CMOS is stuck. Costs no RAM.

config DRV_RTC_PCF8563
    # cargo-feature: rtc-pcf8563
    bool "RTC: NXP PCF8563 on I2C (boards)"
    depends on DRV_RTC && !ARCH_X86_64
    default n
    help
      Kernel feature `rtc-pcf8563`: for a board with no memory-mapped RTC,
      look for a PCF8563 at address 0x51 on bus RTC_PCF8563_I2C_BUS once
      the I2C controllers are up (`install_robot_hw`) and seed the wall
      clock from it. The kernel's I2C driver is the transport; QEMU has no
      such chip, so the boot prints that nothing answers. Board validation
      pending.

config RTC_PCF8563_I2C_BUS
    int "RTC: I2C bus the PCF8563 sits on"
    depends on DRV_RTC_PCF8563
    range 0 255
    default 0
    help
      The `I2c` driver names a device by bus and address; the address is
      the chip's fixed 0x51, the bus number is the I2C driver's own
      numbering (crates/drivers/bus/src/i2c.rs).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Driver placement (wave 11 DRVPLACE)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# One chip-logic source per driver, two hosts: in the kernel (a direct call
# in the caller's context) or in ring 3 (a driver process behind the
# kernel's proxy). Chosen per driver here, for the board this .config is
# for; later also by the signed topology. Only drivers whose chip logic has
# been separated from its host have a choice (INA219, buzzer).
#
# Closed rule: a driver a safety decision reads never
# goes to ring 3, so it has no choice here and always runs in the kernel:
# clint/plic, wdt, pwm/gpio/motor/motor_pid/esc (actuation gate, rt_motor,
# flight control, e-stop pin), rc (flight failsafe), i2c with the IMU
# (attitude, imu_valid), rangefinder (obstacle check), ads1115 (battery
# check), uart (console, panic path), virtio-blk/mmc (e-stop latch replay,
# crash log). tools/kconfig_to_cargo.py refuses a .config that places any
# of them in ring 3. Drivers of Linux origin always run in ring 3 (none is
# in the tree yet).

config DRV_RING3_DEFAULT
    bool "Place drivers that support it in ring 3 by default"
    default n
    help
      The starting value of every DRV_<NAME>_PLACEMENT below becomes
      `ring3`. Implied by STRUCTURE_RING3_DRIVERS; each driver's choice can
      still be changed by hand. n: each driver keeps its own default
      (written in its help). Drivers with no placement choice are not
      affected (see the closed rule above).

choice DRV_INA219_PLACEMENT
    prompt "INA219 power monitor: placement"
    # A placement choice whose own default is not ring 3 carries
    # `default <ring3 member> if DRV_RING3_DEFAULT` first
    # (STRUCTURE_RING3_DRIVERS starts it in ring 3). This one needs none:
    # its own default is ring 3 (no safety decision reads it), the same as
    # a build with no cargo feature.
    default DRV_INA219_PLACEMENT_RING3
    help
      Where the INA219 chip logic (crates/drivers/ina219) runs. Both
      answer a read of the `sensor.9` endpoint (the power monitor) with the same
      record.
      Not read by any safety decision (the battery check reads the
      ADS1115), so both are allowed.

    config DRV_INA219_PLACEMENT_KERNEL
        # cargo-feature: ina219-kernel base
        bool "kernel"
        help
          Kernel feature `ina219-kernel`: a kernel task samples the chip
          at 10 Hz and the kernel API reads its last sample directly (no
          request to a driver process, no wake-up). The topology declares
          no INADRV.ELF row. A chip fault is a kernel fault.

    config DRV_INA219_PLACEMENT_RING3
        bool "ring3"
        help
          The ring-3 driver INADRV.ELF (userspace/drivers/ina_drv), started
          from its topology row, supervised and restarted as the row's
          `restart` key says (on failure by default); the kernel reaches
          it through the driver proxy (PROXY_SPIN_US, PROXY_REPLY_TIMEOUT_MS). Only images that carry INADRV.ELF and
          start its row have the driver (today: the QEMU drivers disks).

endchoice

choice DRV_BUZZER_PLACEMENT
    prompt "Piezo buzzer: starting placement"
    # Its own default is ring 3 (no safety decision reads it), the same as
    # a build with no cargo feature, so no DRV_RING3_DEFAULT line.
    default DRV_BUZZER_PLACEMENT_RING3
    help
      The host the buzzer chip logic (crates/drivers/buzzer) starts in. It is
      the `place` of the built-in topology's BUZZDRV.ELF row; a signed
      topology says its own. The row is `stateless`, so the driver can change
      host while the system runs (azos_topology::placement): from the kernel
      to ring 3 when the kernel host faults, from ring 3 to the kernel when
      its measured call cost breaks DRV_BUZZER_CALL_BUDGET_US. Both hosts play
      the same PWM channel state (channel 5, 50% duty) and return once the
      first step of the sound has started. Not read by any safety decision, so
      both are allowed.

    config DRV_BUZZER_PLACEMENT_KERNEL
        # cargo-feature: buzzer-kernel base
        bool "kernel"
        help
          Kernel feature `buzzer-kernel`: the driver starts in the kernel. A
          call plays through the kernel's PWM driver in the caller's context
          (no message), and a kernel task advances the timed steps. A fault
          in the chip logic is contained, and the calls go to the ring-3 host,
          which the component manager keeps up, idle, beside the kernel one.

    config DRV_BUZZER_PLACEMENT_RING3
        bool "ring3"
        help
          The driver starts as the ring-3 program BUZZDRV.ELF
          (userspace/drivers/buzz_drv), started from its topology row and
          supervised by the component manager; the kernel calls it on the
          endpoint it serves. Only
          images that carry BUZZDRV.ELF and start its row have the driver
          (today: the QEMU drivers disks).

endchoice

config DRV_BUZZER_CALL_BUDGET_US
    int "Piezo buzzer: call budget (microseconds, 0: none)"
    range 0 1000000
    default 0
    help
      The `call_budget_us` of the built-in topology's BUZZDRV.ELF row: how
      long one call to the buzzer may take, from the caller. In ring 3, when
      PLACEMENT_PROMOTE_MISSES of the last 16 calls take longer, the driver
      is moved into the kernel if the kernel host's measured cost fits the
      budget (the move or its refusal is a placement decision record). 0, the
      default, declares no budget: the driver is never moved for cost. A
      signed topology declares its own.

config DEVICE_WAKELOCKS
    int "Device runtime PM: wakelocks live at once, per device"
    range 1 8
    default 4
    help
      The quota of the wakelock ledger every ring-3 driver that serves the
      DevicePower interface keeps (INADRV.ELF, GPIODRV.ELF): how many leases
      one device has live at once. A lease keeps the device from suspending
      until it is released or lapses; a hold past the quota is refused (BUSY)
      and recorded, so one client cannot pin a device awake.

config DEVICE_WAKELOCK_TTL_MAX_MS
    int "Device runtime PM: longest wakelock (milliseconds)"
    range 1 3600000
    default 60000
    help
      The longest deadline a device grants a wakelock for. A holder that dies
      or forgets lapses at its deadline (recorded); a longer ttl is refused
      (BAD_ARGUMENT). The idle timeout of each device is policy, not
      configuration: POWER.ELF applies the energy preset's.

config ADS1115_PIPELINE
    bool "ADS1115 ADC: pipelined battery reading (no conversion polling)"
    default y
    help
      crates/drivers/sensor/src/ads1115.rs, ads1115_read_battery_mv (the
      10 Hz sensor_slow_task and SYS_SENSOR's battery). y: each read takes
      the conversion the previous one started (its ready bit, its result)
      and starts the next one: at most 3 I2C transactions, never a poll,
      the value one period (100 ms) older; the first read after boot has no
      value (battery 0 = "no reading", which every safety check skips).
      n: start a conversion and poll its ready bit, up to
      ADS1115_CONVERSION_POLL_ITERS locked I2C reads in the sensor task
      (about 8 ms of bus at 128 SPS, 125 ms at 8 SPS). Costs no RAM either
      way; the same on every ISA. Say n only to debug a board whose ADS1115
      loses the started conversion between periods.

config ADS1115_CONVERSION_POLL_ITERS
    int "ADS1115 ADC: most config-register polls of one blocking read"
    range 1000 10000000
    default 150000
    help
      crates/drivers/sensor/src/ads1115.rs, CONVERSION_POLL_ITERS: the
      blocking read (ads1115_read_mv, and the battery when
      ADS1115_PIPELINE=n) polls the conversion-ready bit at most this many
      times, each a locked I2C read, before it gives up with no value. A
      conversion takes 125 ms at 8 SPS; raise it only for an I2C bus fast
      enough that 150000 reads take less than that. Costs no RAM; bounds
      the read's worst case at this many bus transactions.

config CAMERA_RING_SLOTS
    int "Camera frame ring: slots"
    default 4 if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default 4 if (HWCLASS_SBC || HWCLASS_DESKTOP)
    default 8 if HWCLASS_SERVER
    default 4
    range 4 16
    help
      Wave 15 (B2). One capture task captures and JPEG-encodes each camera
      frame once into this ring (crates/drivers/cam-ring, FanoutRing); the
      brain link's sender (camera-tx, or the behavior task's inline path)
      reads it. The JPEG is encoded once per frame.

      Cost: SLOTS x 19,200 B of .bss (the JPEG bound, csi::JPEG_MAX_SIZE)
      on images with the robot domain; 4 slots = 76,800 B. With USB_UVC a
      slot also holds a raw YUY2 frame of the JPEG path's size, 38,400 B
      (4 slots = 153,600 B). Per frame the
      producer scans the slots twice (a few loads each).

      The minimum, 4, is what the ring needs for the sender never to tear a
      frame and the producer never to wait: the slot pinned by the sender,
      the newest frame, the one being written, and one spare. More slots
      only matter under CAMERA_RING_BACKPRESSURE, where they are frames a
      slow sender may fall behind before capture stops; with the default
      overwrite policy the sender reads the newest frame. The same on
      every ISA.

choice CAMERA_RING_POLICY
    prompt "Camera frame ring: when a consumer lags"
    default CAMERA_RING_OVERWRITE_OLDEST
    help
      What the capture task does when every free slot of the camera frame
      ring holds a frame the attached sender has not taken yet.

config CAMERA_RING_OVERWRITE_OLDEST
    bool "Overwrite the oldest frame (the latest frame wins)"
    help
      Capture never waits on a consumer: a slow TCP sender loses the frames
      overwritten meanwhile (counted) and takes the newest one next, with
      its own acquisition stamp.

config CAMERA_RING_BACKPRESSURE
    bool "Back-pressure (capture waits for the slowest consumer)"
    help
      The producer refuses a frame (counted, no capture) rather than
      overwrite one the attached sender is owed. A sender stuck behind a
      closed TCP window then holds back capture. Only for a sender that must
      see every frame.

endchoice

config CAMERA_TX_CAPTURE_PERIOD_MS
    int "Camera frame ring: capture period for the brain link's sender"
    default 250
    range 10 1000
    help
      The capture period of the camera ring while the brain link's camera
      sender is attached (with no sender attached nothing is captured).
      The sender takes the newest frame at most every 500 ms
      (azos_behavior::camera_tx::FRAME_PERIOD_MS, or the behavior task's
      ~2 Hz inline path); capturing at half that period means each send
      finds a frame no older than this. Cost: one capture + JPEG encode
      per period. Raise it to save CPU; the frame it sends gets older by as
      much. The same on every profile and ISA today: the sender's 500 ms
      period is not per profile. With no sender attached the capture task
      does not wake at all.

menu "I2C bus transactions"

config I2C_DESIGNWARE
    # cargo-feature: i2c-dw
    bool "I2C: Synopsys DesignWare controllers (VisionFive 2, Raspberry Pi 5 RP1, Intel LPSS)"
    depends on !BOARD_K1
    default y if BOARD_VF2 || BOARD_RPI5
    default n
    help
      Kernel feature `i2c-dw` (crates/drivers/bus/src/i2c_dw.rs and
      i2c_txn.rs). Drives every Synopsys DesignWare APB I2C controller the
      hardware bus finds: a device-tree node `snps,designware-i2c` (the
      JH7110's, and the RP1's behind the Raspberry Pi 5's PCIe), and an
      Intel LPSS I2C function by PCI id (crates/drivers/bus/hwbus.ids). Each
      is checked to be one (IC_COMP_TYPE) and brought up as a master from
      what the firmware says: the input clock (the node's clock, the PCI
      id's), the bus rate (`clock-frequency`: 100 kHz, 400 kHz or 1 MHz),
      the fall and SDA hold times; the FIFO depth and fastest mode from the
      IP's IC_COMP_PARAM_1. With no input clock known, the SCL counts the
      controller holds are kept and the boot line says so. Selected by
      BOARD_VF2 and BOARD_RPI5 (their I2C is this IP); on a PC
      (BOARD_GENERIC) it is the choice for an Intel board. It replaces the
      QEMU simulation of the IMU, barometer and INA219: QEMU has no
      DesignWare controller, so a QEMU build with it has no I2C device. AMD's
      FCH controllers (ACPI `AMDI0010`) are the same IP but are found only
      in the DSDT, which the kernel does not walk yet. Board validation
      pending.

config I2C_TXN_QUEUE_DEPTH
    int "I2C: queued transactions per bus"
    range 2 64
    default 2 if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default 8 if HWCLASS_SERVER
    default 4
    help
      A caller that may not wait for the
      wire (the real-time `imu` task) queues an I2C transaction and
      collects it later (crates/drivers/bus/src/i2c_txn.rs); this many wait
      per bus, and this many finished results stay readable. A submission
      to a full queue is refused and counted, never waited for. Costs about
      (16 + 48) bytes per entry per bus (4 buses). Raise it when several
      tasks queue on one bus.

config I2C_DW_RX_FIFO_DEPTH
    int "I2C: DesignWare controller RX FIFO depth (entries)"
    range 2 256
    default 8
    help
      The RX FIFO depth of a DesignWare controller built without its
      encoded parameters (IC_COMP_PARAM_1 reads 0); every other one reports
      its own depth (bits 15:8, plus one), which is used instead. Read
      commands in flight never exceed the depth, so the controller's
      receive FIFO cannot overflow; it also sets the RX interrupt
      threshold. Must not exceed the hardware's depth: a larger value lets
      reads overrun the FIFO. Board validation pending: QEMU has no
      DesignWare I2C.

config I2C_K1_FAST_MODE
    bool "I2C: SpacemiT K1 controllers in fast mode (400 kHz)"
    depends on BOARD_K1
    default y
    help
      The K1's I2C controllers (`spacemit,k1-i2c`, crates/drivers/bus/src/
      i2c_k1.rs) run at 400 kHz, the rate the mainline device tree gives
      every node (`clock-frequency = <400000>`), when this is y, and at
      100 kHz (standard mode) when n. The controller derives SCL from its
      functional clock and this one mode bit; there is no divisor to
      program. Set n for a bus whose slowest device cannot do fast mode.
      Board validation pending (SpacemiT K1).

config I2C_XFER_TIMEOUT_US
    int "I2C: queued transaction timeout (us)"
    range 500 1000000
    default 5000
    help
      A queued transaction not finished this long after it started is
      ended as failed (counted); a stuck bus then feeds the sensor's
      staleness check, never a wait. The IMU's 14-byte read is ~1.35 ms of
      wire at 100 kHz. No RAM or instruction cost.

config I2C_SERVICE_POLL_US
    int "I2C: service step period while a transaction is on the wire (us)"
    range 50 10000
    default 200
    help
      The `i2c-svc` task (VisionFive 2) runs one bounded controller step
      this often while its bus has work, sleeping in between, until the
      controller's interrupt line is wired (then the interrupt runs the
      same step). A synchronous caller sleeps this long between the steps
      of its own transfer; preemption is off only inside a step. With an 8-entry FIFO a 14-byte read needs about two
      refills; shorter: lower latency, more timer wakes. Idle buses cost
      nothing (the submit wakes the task). The SpacemiT K1 controller has
      no FIFO here: a step moves ONE byte, so a transaction takes about
      (bytes + 2) steps and this period is the gap between bytes (the
      controller holds SCL low meanwhile); keep it near one byte time at the
      bus rate (config/defconfigs/robot-sbc-riscv64+k1.config sets the minimum).

config DEVIF_PUMP_POLL_US
    int "device pump: look again at a source with no interrupt after (us)"
    range 100 1000000
    default 1000
    help
      The `dev-pump` task moves data between the queues a client attached
      (`Uart::attach`, `Adc::start_batch`, `Sound::open`) and the adapters
      behind them. It
      is created by the first attach, so a machine where nothing attaches
      pays no stack and no task slot for it. A
      source that raises no interrupt is looked at again this long after
      the last pass: the UART bridge's line and a plugged USB serial line
      (and with it the USB serial console) while they are attached, and an
      ADC channel whose conversion has not answered yet (a sample that IS due
      later is waited for to the nanosecond, not rounded to this). Cost: one
      task wake per period while a ring is attached; nothing otherwise (the
      attach wakes the task). Latency of received bytes: up to one period,
      or one scheduler tick if that is longer. A sound stream's `ready` queue
      has no doorbell on the kernel side, so a period the client posts reaches
      the device within this long (the device's own interrupt wakes the task
      for the completions).

config I2C_SERVICE_PRIORITY
    int "I2C: priority of the i2c-svc task"
    range 12 30
    default 12
    help
      Highest non-real-time priority by default: it serves the real-time
      IMU's queued reads, and it waits (sleeps) between steps, so it may
      not be in the RT band (below 12). A synchronous transfer (sensor
      init, the typed I2C syscalls, the AHRS) steps its own transaction
      and sleeps I2C_SERVICE_POLL_US between steps, so it does not depend
      on this task.

choice
    prompt "IMU sample source"
    default IMU_SAMPLE_QUEUED
    help
      How the real-time `imu` task gets its 100 Hz sample.

config IMU_SAMPLE_QUEUED
    bool "Queued read, re-armed from the task's tick (no wait)"
    help
      The task queues the 14-byte burst (I2C_TXN_QUEUE_DEPTH) and
      publishes the finished one on a later tick, stamped at acquisition;
      it never waits for the wire (owner rule: RT tasks only enqueue). On
      the QEMU simulation the read completes at submit.

config IMU_SAMPLE_POLLED
    bool "Synchronous read in the task (before wave 15)"
    help
      The task does a synchronous transfer and sleeps until it is done
      (~1.35 ms per read at 100 kHz on the real bus): the RT task waits
      for I/O. Only for comparison.

endchoice

endmenu # I2C bus transactions

menu "DMA and IOMMU"

# Every device that does DMA reaches memory through a DMA domain
# (crates/drivers/dma): the domain turns a buffer into the bus address the
# device is told, may refuse the buffer, and, with an IOMMU behind it, makes
# the hardware refuse every page that was not mapped. The users today are the
# virtio drivers (block, net, rng, input); the boot installs the domains
# (kernel/src/boot/dma.rs).

choice
    prompt "DMA domain of a device with no IOMMU in front of it"
    default DMA_DOMAIN_SOFT
    help
      Every virtio-mmio device, and every PCI function when no IOMMU is
      driven (the IOMMU_* switches below), maps its buffers through this.

    config DMA_DOMAIN_SOFT
        bool "software-checked: each mapping must lie in RAM and in the device's reach"
        help
          A mapping outside the RAM the kernel manages, or beyond what the
          device can address, is refused, and every live mapping is recorded
          so an unmap of something never mapped is caught. It stops a driver
          bug that would hand a device the wrong page. It cannot stop a
          device that ignores its descriptors: only an IOMMU does.

    config DMA_DOMAIN_IDENTITY
        bool "identity: the device is handed physical addresses, unchecked"
        help
          What every driver did before domains existed. No bookkeeping and
          no checks; the mapping calls cost one addition each.
endchoice

config DMA_MAP_ENTRIES
    int "Live DMA mappings per domain"
    range 8 1024
    default 128
    help
      Each static buffer a driver hands to a device is one mapping for the
      life of the kernel: virtio-blk takes 3 (headers, status bytes, staging
      buffers) plus its ring, virtio-net 3 plus 2 rings, rng and input about
      2 each. The software-checked domain and every IOMMU domain keep this
      many records. Cost: 24 bytes of .bss per record per domain.

config DMA_NONCOHERENT
    bool "Devices are NOT cache-coherent with the CPU (cache maintenance around every DMA transfer)"
    default y if BOARD_VF2
    default n
    depends on ARCH_RISCV64 || ARCH_AARCH64
    help
      Set for a machine whose DMA masters read and write RAM behind the
      CPU's caches. This is the platform default: a device whose node in
      the device tree has `dma-coherent` (or sits behind a bus that has
      it) is recorded as coherent by the hardware bus and skips the
      maintenance. Every other driver then cleans the cache lines of a buffer
      before the device reads it, and flushes them around a transfer the
      device writes (crates/drivers/dma/src/cache.rs): riscv64 Zicbom
      `cbo.clean` / `cbo.flush` (needs RV_ZICBOM probe or require), else the
      `Flush64` line flush of the SiFive composable cache controller the
      device tree names (`sifive,ccache0`, `starfive,jh7110-ccache`: the
      VisionFive 2's U74 has no Zicbom; crates/drivers/dma/src/ccache.rs),
      else the boot refuses; aarch64 `dc cvac` / `dc civac`. On by default
      for BOARD_VF2. The virtqueue rings and
      the buffers are laid out so a cache line never holds bytes of two
      owners. With an IOMMU (IOMMU_*) the unit is taken not to snoop either:
      the tables it reads are cleaned after every store, and the queues it
      writes (fault and event queues, the AMD-Vi completion semaphore) are
      cleaned once before the unit is enabled and invalidated before every
      read. x86_64 DMA is coherent and has no switch. n: the devices are
      coherent (QEMU; no cache maintenance, no cost).

# The IOMMU is on wherever the machine has one: each flavour below is built
# into every kernel of its ISA that has PCI, and finds its unit at boot (a PCI
# function, the device tree, the ACPI tables). Nothing is hardcoded: a machine
# whose firmware describes no unit runs without one and says so. IOMMU_BYPASS
# is the one opt-out from confinement.

config IOMMU_RISCV
    # cargo-feature: iommu-riscv base
    bool "RISC-V IOMMU (riscv-iommu-pci): confine PCI DMA"
    default y
    depends on DRV_PCI && ARCH_RISCV64
    help
      Kernel feature `iommu-riscv` (crates/drivers/iommu/src/riscv.rs).
      Finds the PCI function of class 0x0806 the hardware bus lists
      (`riscv-iommu`, crates/drivers/iommu/hwbus.ids; QEMU
      `-device riscv-iommu-pci`), assigns its BAR, brings it up with a
      two-level device directory and polled command and fault queues, and
      puts each PCI function in the domain its driver needs
      (kernel/src/boot/dma.rs): a translating domain of its own when its
      driver maps its DMA, pass-through when its driver does not (NVMe,
      AHCI), blocked when no kernel driver binds it. From then on a DMA to a
      page the driver did not map is refused and the fault is logged
      (`[IOMMU]`). A machine without the function runs without an IOMMU.
      n: no IOMMU is driven.

config IOMMU_SMMUV3
    # cargo-feature: iommu-smmuv3 base
    bool "Arm SMMUv3 (QEMU virt iommu=smmuv3): confine PCI DMA"
    default y
    depends on DRV_PCI && ARCH_AARCH64
    help
      Kernel feature `iommu-smmuv3` (crates/drivers/iommu/src/smmuv3.rs).
      Finds the SMMU in the device tree (`arm,smmu-v3`, its `reg` window;
      crates/drivers/iommu/hwbus.ids) and brings it up with a linear stream
      table and polled command and event queues; the functions get their
      domains as with IOMMU_RISCV. The stream id is the PCI requester id
      (QEMU `virt` maps them one to one). A tree without the node runs
      without an IOMMU. n: no IOMMU is driven.

config IOMMU_VTD
    # cargo-feature: iommu-vtd base
    bool "Intel VT-d (QEMU q35 intel-iommu): confine PCI DMA"
    default y
    depends on DRV_PCI && ARCH_X86_64
    help
      Kernel feature `iommu-vtd` (crates/drivers/iommu/src/vtd.rs). The
      remapping unit's registers are found in the ACPI DMAR table (the first
      DRHD that covers PCI segment 0), brought up in legacy mode with
      register-based invalidation and polled fault recording; the functions
      get their domains as with IOMMU_RISCV. Without a DMAR the kernel runs
      without it (and tries IOMMU_AMDVI). n: no VT-d unit is driven.

config IOMMU_AMDVI
    # cargo-feature: iommu-amdvi base
    bool "AMD-Vi: confine PCI DMA"
    default y
    depends on DRV_PCI && ARCH_X86_64
    help
      Kernel feature `iommu-amdvi` (crates/drivers/iommu/src/amdvi.rs). The
      unit's registers are found in the ACPI IVRS table (the first IVHD
      block), brought up with a flat device table, every entry of which
      blocks until its function is placed, and polled command buffer and
      event log; the functions get their domains as with IOMMU_RISCV. QEMU
      q35 with `-device amd-iommu,dma-remap=on` models the DMA page walk.
      Without an IVRS the kernel runs without it. n: no AMD-Vi unit is
      driven.

config IOMMU_BYPASS
    bool "IOMMU in pass-through: do NOT confine any device's DMA"
    default n
    depends on IOMMU_RISCV || IOMMU_SMMUV3 || IOMMU_VTD || IOMMU_AMDVI
    help
      The unit is still found and brought up, but every PCI function is put
      in pass-through: no translating domain, no blocked function, and a
      device can read and write all of memory, the kernel's included (what
      Linux `iommu.passthrough=1` does). The boot log says so per function.
      For measuring what confinement costs, or for a device a driver cannot
      yet map through its domain. n (the default): every function is
      confined as IOMMU_RISCV describes.

config IOMMU_DOMAINS
    int "Translating PCI functions at once (with an IOMMU_* unit)"
    range 1 16
    default 8
    help
      With an IOMMU_* unit, each PCI function whose driver maps its DMA
      through its domain (virtio-net/gpu/snd over PCI, e1000e, igc, xHCI)
      gets a translating domain of its own, up to this many; a function past
      the last one stays in pass-through and the boot log says so. At most 16:
      the domain registry (azos_dma::registry::MAX_BOUND) and the units' tag
      allocators hold 16. Cost: one DMA_MAP_ENTRIES record table (about 32
      bytes per record) of .bss per domain, and a page-table root page per
      translating function at boot.

config IOMMU_FAULT_POLL_MS
    int "IOMMU fault log poll interval (ms; with an IOMMU_* unit)"
    range 10 10000
    default 100
    help
      With an IOMMU_* unit, a kernel task wakes this often, drains the unit's fault queue (the
      units' interrupts stay off) and prints one `[IOMMU] fault` line per
      refused DMA, up to 16 per wake. One MMIO read per wake when nothing
      faulted.

endmenu # DMA and IOMMU

comment "Always compiled, no switch: FAT32/tmpfs, the network stack, the CSI driver, native drivers"
